(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-micro-exam-2-webster-review-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Webster Review Quiz 2 — Microbiology Exam 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which failure of the immune system allows cancer to develop?","choices":["An overreaction to self antigens","An exaggerated response to an allergen","Immune complexes lodging in tissue","A lack of immune surveillance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: cancer is an underreaction, reached through a lack of surveillance, because immune surveillance is what normally keeps cancer in check. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 3-4, 66"},{"q":"A positive tuberculin skin test leaves a raised, reddened area of skin that is measured with calipers. Which mechanism produces this reaction?","choices":["Immune complexes lodged in basement membranes","Activation of and damage by T cells","Antibody and complement lysing target cells","Immunoglobulin E crosslinked on mast cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the tuberculin skin test is a type IV, delayed hypersensitivity response, in which T cells are activated and damage the tissue; no antibody is involved. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 40-41"},{"q":"Immunoglobulin G and M initiate both type II and type III hypersensitivity. Which feature marks a reaction as type II?","choices":["The antibody binds a cell-surface antigen","The antibody binds a soluble antigen","A large amount of antigen is required","Symptoms are delayed hours to days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in type II free antibody binds antigens on a cell surface, complement is activated and the membrane attack complex lyses the cell, as in transfusion reactions. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 9, 32-38"},{"q":"How do antihistamines reduce the symptoms of a type I allergic reaction?","choices":["They bind histamine receptors on target organs","They inhibit lymphocytes to reduce immunoglobulin E","They block degranulation of the mast cells","They are an antibody against immunoglobulin E"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: antihistamines bind the histamine receptors on target organs, so histamine released by mast cells cannot act there; they are one of the pharmacological controls. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient with type A blood is given type B red cells by mistake, and the donor cells are rapidly destroyed. What destroys them?","choices":["Recipient T cells against histocompatibility molecules","The donor's anti-A antibody in the plasma","The recipient's anti-B antibody and complement","Immunoglobulin E on the recipient's mast cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a type A recipient carries anti-B antibody against the antigen it lacks; it binds the donor type B cells, complement is activated and the cells lyse, which can be fatal. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 33-34"},{"q":"A man with type AB, Rh-negative blood has never received a transfusion. Which antibodies would his serum be expected to contain?","choices":["Anti-Rh antibody only","Anti-A, anti-B and anti-Rh","Anti-A and anti-B antibodies","No antibody against A, B or Rh"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: type AB cells carry both A and B, so the serum has no antibody against either, and an Rh-negative person makes no anti-Rh antibody unless sensitized by transfusion or pregnancy. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 33-36"},{"q":"Donor cells from a transplant attack the recipient's skin, liver and gastrointestinal tract. Which transplant most likely caused this?","choices":["Cornea","Bone marrow","Liver","Kidney"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: bone marrow is the transplant most sensitive to human leukocyte antigen discrepancies, which can cause graft versus host disease, a systemic attack, in about 30% of recipients. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 48-49"},{"q":"Weeks after a bout of Campylobacter diarrhea, a patient develops arthritis as the immune response turns on joint tissue. Which origin of autoimmunity does this illustrate?","choices":["A noninfectious response","Molecular mimicry","Thymic senescence","Sequestered antigen release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: reactive arthritis after Shigella, Salmonella or Campylobacter results from a normal immune response, molecular mimicry, in which similar foreign and self antigens cross-react. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 53-57"},{"q":"A child with agammaglobulinemia has no antibodies. Which kind of recurrent infection is most typical?","choices":["Bacterial","Protozoan","Fungal","Viral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: agammaglobulinemia is a primary B cell defect in which the patient lacks antibodies, and B cell deficiency goes with recurrent bacterial infections. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 63-64"},{"q":"Cells from a tumor in one organ are found growing in a distant organ. Which necessary characteristic of cancer cells does this show?","choices":["Evasion of the immune response","Angiogenesis for a blood supply","Avoidance of death by apoptosis","Metastasis to other tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: cancer cells leave their site of origin to invade other tissues, which is metastasis; a malignant tumor is one that spreads from its tissue of origin to other sites. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 66-69"},{"q":"Chimeric antigen receptor T cells are approved for several blood cancers. What is the status of chimeric antigen receptor natural killer cells?","choices":["Approved for melanoma and some lung cancers","Abandoned after radiation and chemotherapy","Mostly in trials, some on solid tumors","Approved for the same blood cancers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: chimeric antigen receptor genes are inserted into natural killer cells to make them more efficient cancer killers; most are in clinical trials, including trials on solid tumors. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slides 75, 79"},{"q":"In which form of anthrax do about half of infected patients show symptoms of meningitis?","choices":["Injection","Gastrointestinal","Pulmonary","Cutaneous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: in pulmonary anthrax the exotoxins kill the alveolar macrophages that engulfed the inhaled spores, and about half of infected patients have symptoms of meningitis. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 10, 12-15"},{"q":"Which products does the genus Clostridium synthesize?","choices":["Organic acids, alcohols and exotoxins","Mycolic acids and waxes in the wall","Antibiotics such as bacitracin","Catalase and a polypeptide capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Clostridium synthesizes organic acids, alcohols and exotoxins, and its exotoxins are among the most toxic substances on earth. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 7, 9, 19, 67"},{"q":"Which pair of effects comes from the toxins of Clostridium perfringens in myonecrosis?","choices":["Blocked inhibitory transmitters and spasm","Blocked acetylcholine and flaccid paralysis","Myocarditis and nerve damage with paralysis","Red cell rupture and white cell destruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: of its at least 17 toxins, one ruptures red blood cells and causes edema and tissue destruction, and another destroys white blood cells. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 24, 29, 35, 62"},{"q":"Which condition does Clostridium tetani need to germinate its spores and produce toxin?","choices":["Refrigerator temperatures","High oxygen tension","Low-acid surroundings","Low oxygen tension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Clostridium tetani is a strict anaerobe; low oxygen tension is needed to germinate its spores and produce toxin, which is why puncture wounds, burns and crushed tissue suit it. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 29, 34, 55"},{"q":"How is a diagnosis of botulism confirmed in the laboratory?","choices":["Alkaline methylene blue stain of a swab","Acid-fast stain of a sputum sample","Finding toxin in food, gut contents or feces","Cold enrichment culture of the food"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: diagnosis rests on determining the presence of the toxin in the food, intestinal contents or feces, and treatment is antitoxin with cardiac and respiratory support. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 40, 56, 63, 77"},{"q":"A 4-month-old infant fed homemade baby food becomes weak and limp, with flaccid paralysis. Which organism is the likely cause?","choices":["Clostridium botulinum","Listeria monocytogenes","Clostridium tetani","Clostridium perfringens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in infant botulism, ingested spores from honey or homemade baby food germinate and release toxin, causing flaccid paralysis, the floppy baby syndrome. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 29, 31, 33, 38, 41, 55"},{"q":"In fecal microbiota transplant for Clostridioides difficile infection, what do the capsules called poop pills contain?","choices":["Antitoxin made in horse serum","Live Lactobacillus cultures","A purified toxoid vaccine","Donor stool spun into pellets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a donor sample is centrifuged down to pellets and taken as triple-layered capsules, an alternative to giving donor cultures by colonoscope or nasogastric tube. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 16, 48, 51, 64"},{"q":"Which rapid diagnostic tests are used for Listeria monocytogenes?","choices":["Alkaline methylene blue stain and serological assay","Detecting the toxin in food, gut contents or feces","Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay and immunofluorescence","Ziehl-Neelsen stain and tuberculin skin testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: besides the lengthy cold enrichment culture, rapid diagnosis uses enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, immunofluorescence and deoxyribonucleic acid analysis. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 40, 56, 63, 76, 77"},{"q":"Deli meat contaminated with Listeria monocytogenes is kept in a refrigerator for a week. What happens to the bacteria?","choices":["They can keep growing in the cold","They are killed by the cold","They survive but cannot multiply","They form spores and lie dormant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Listeria is resistant to cold and can contaminate foods and grow during refrigeration, so prevention relies on pasteurization and cooking. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 55, 56"},{"q":"Which limitation do diphtheria antitoxin and tetanus immune globulin share?","choices":["Both must be repeated every 10 years","Neither neutralizes toxin already bound","Both are given mainly for skin infections","Both are prepared in horse serum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: both neutralize circulating toxin, which prevents further spread, but neither counteracts toxin that is already bound to its targets. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 32, 64"},{"q":"The pseudomembrane is the characteristic sign of respiratory diphtheria. What forms it?","choices":["Necrotic lining that sloughs off","Caseous necrosis that later calcifies","A biofilm of bacteria on the tonsil","Fluid from inflammation that solidifies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the diphtheria pseudomembrane forms from the solidification of fluids from inflammation in the upper respiratory tract; with swelling it is indicative, and it can cause asphyxiation. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 46, 62, 63, 66, 73"},{"q":"Which oxygen requirement describes Cutibacterium acnes?","choices":["Strictly aerobic","Aerotolerant or anaerobic","Obligately anaerobic spore-former","Aerobic or facultatively anaerobic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Cutibacterium acnes is a Gram-positive rod that is aerotolerant or anaerobic, and diagnosing an implant infection needs prolonged incubation under strict anaerobic conditions. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 5, 19, 65, 66, 67"},{"q":"Which kind of immune response does primary tuberculosis stimulate?","choices":["An antitoxin response to exotoxin","A cell-mediated response","An immediate allergic response","A mainly humoral antibody response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: primary tuberculosis stimulates a cell-mediated response; after 3 to 4 weeks the immune system attacks and forms tubercles, granulomas of bacilli surrounded by white blood cells. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 70, 73, 76"},{"q":"Which feature describes tuberculoid leprosy?","choices":["Few organisms and low infectivity","Florid bacterial growth in macrophages","Low or absent T-cell response","Deep nodules that disfigure the face"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in tuberculoid leprosy organisms are present at low to undetectable levels and infectivity is low, with granulomas, local inflammation and peripheral nerve damage. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 83, 84, 86"},{"q":"Which form of actinomycosis is a necrotizing lung disorder?","choices":["Abdominal","Uterine","Thoracic","Cervicofacial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: thoracic actinomycosis is a necrotizing lung disorder, one of the chronic deeper-tissue infections Actinomyces israelii causes once it leaves its normal sites. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Pulmonary nocardiosis lesions may extend through the chest wall. To which organs can they disseminate?","choices":["Heart and peripheral nerves","Long bones and genital tract","Skin and Schwann cells","Brain and kidneys"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in the tuberculosis-like pulmonary disease of Nocardia, lesions may extend through the chest wall and disseminate to the brain and kidneys. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 62, 75, 83, 94"},{"q":"Which two organisms normally live in the human body and cause disease when they overgrow or reach deeper tissue?","choices":["Clostridium tetani and Bacillus anthracis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Mycobacterium leprae","Clostridioides difficile and Actinomyces israelii","Listeria monocytogenes and Corynebacterium diphtheriae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Clostridioides difficile is a colon resident that overgrows after broad-spectrum antibiotics, and Actinomyces israelii is an oral, digestive and genital resident that causes disease on reaching deeper tissue. — Lecture 9 Dr. Webster Gram positive Bacilli.pptx, Slides 8, 13, 28, 29, 42, 55, 61, 71, 82, 93"},{"q":"Investigators studying a respiratory infection record household crowding, housing quality and sewage disposal in each home. In the triad that analytic epidemiology assesses, where do these factors belong?","choices":["Environment","Agent","Place","Host"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the analytic triad is host, agent and environment, and living conditions such as housing, crowding, water supply, refuse and sewage are environmental factors. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 37-40"},{"q":"Days after a cluster of severe pneumonia cases appears in one town, an epidemiologist identifies it, reports it to the health department and investigates it. Which use of epidemiological training is this?","choices":["Program evaluation and management","Outbreak response","Disease prioritization","Public health communication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: outbreak response means identifying outbreaks, reporting them and investigating them in a timely manner, as done here within days. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 7-8"},{"q":"The multigenerational study that began in Framingham in 1948 set out to identify what?","choices":["Common factors in cardiovascular disease","The water source of cholera outbreaks","The link between smoking and lung cancer","Mortality gaps between town and country"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the Framingham Heart Study, begun in 1948, is a multigenerational study to identify common factors contributing to cardiovascular disease. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 10-11"},{"q":"Genomic epidemiology is called one of the most reliable tools in which application of epidemiology?","choices":["Environmental issues","Policy development","Outbreak investigation","Occupational health"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: genomic epidemiology is one of the most reliable tools in outbreak investigation, identifying sources of exposure and transmission links and linking cases found in different places. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 12, 61, 69, 72, 76"},{"q":"Social media can be a valuable source of data for monitoring what?","choices":["Cancer cases for state registries","Disease outbreaks and mental health","Cigarette sales across a region","Air and water pollutant levels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: social media can be a valuable source of data for monitoring disease outbreaks and mental health issues. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 18, 20, 22"},{"q":"A surveillance team tightens its case definition so that persons who do not have the disease are no longer counted as cases. Which critical characteristic of surveillance does this improve?","choices":["Sensitivity","Representation","Specificity","Timeliness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: specificity is the critical characteristic of surveillance that excludes persons not having the disease from the count. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How many times a year is the list of notifiable diseases produced?","choices":["1","4","12","52"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a list of notifiable diseases is produced every year, naming the diseases required by law to be reported to government authorities. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 21, 22, 24"},{"q":"A health department counts every death from any cause in its whole population during one year. Which rate does this give?","choices":["Case fatality rate","Cause-specific mortality rate","Age-specific mortality rate","Crude mortality rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the crude mortality rate counts deaths from all causes in a population in a given time period. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 29, 59"},{"q":"A town has 5,000 residents. On 1 January, 300 of them are living with a chronic disease; during the year, 100 more are newly diagnosed, and no one with the disease dies, recovers or moves away. What is the prevalence of the disease in the town during that year?","choices":["2%","6%","8%","33%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the numerator of prevalence is all cases present during the period, 300 existing plus 100 new, so 400 of the 5,000 residents is 8%. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 27-28"},{"q":"Investigators weigh several lines of evidence that an exposure causes a disease. Which one is regarded as one of the most reliable indicators of causality?","choices":["A single characteristic effect","Risk that rises with dose","An association in one study","A plausible mechanism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a biological gradient, in which changes in exposure are related to a trend in relative risk, is one of the most reliable indicators of causality. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 43, 47, 49, 51, 52"},{"q":"Cases of an intestinal illness among workers at one factory, plotted by date of onset, appear on most days for six weeks with no single sharp peak and no successive waves, and stop soon after the factory's contaminated drinking-water tank is replaced. Which type of epidemic does this curve show?","choices":["Propagated","Mixed","Point common source","Continuous common source"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a group exposed to the same source over a longer time gives a continuous common-source curve, with cases spread over weeks until the source is removed. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slides 55-56"},{"q":"In a communicable disease outbreak investigation, at which point is descriptive epidemiology used to describe and orient the data?","choices":["After control methods are implemented","After cases are defined and identified","Before the outbreak is established","After the hypothesis is refined"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: once cases are defined and identified, descriptive epidemiology is used to describe and orient the data, and a focused hypothesis is developed next. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
